"""Rig and animate realistic heroes generated by TRELLIS 2 (tools/fal/gen3d.py hero-model / weapon).

    ~/toolchains/blender/blender -b --factory-startup --python tools/blender/realistic_heroes.py -- <gen dir> [hero...]

<gen dir> holds, per hero: <id>.glb (TRELLIS mesh), <id>_<seed>.jpg + _pose.json (the concept painting
and its joints from tools/fal/pose_keypoints.py), and the weapon meshes (<id>_staff.glb, warden_sword.glb,
warden_shield.glb). For each hero:
  1. facing: TRELLIS meshes face glTF +Z (Blender -Y); turned to face +X like every game model
  2. joints: the painting's 2D joints are mapped onto the mesh through the silhouette's width
     (same scale both ways), and each joint's depth is the middle of the mesh cross-section there
  3. scale: nose-to-ankle set to the game's proportions (heroes ~50 units), feet on 0
  4. rig: the 21-bone skeleton of build_models.humanoid, fitted to those joints, bound with Blender's
     automatic (heat) weights, nearest-bone weights for any vertex heat weighting misses
  5. weapons: TRELLIS weapon meshes stood up by shape (staff head up, sword hilt to the hand, shield
     face out) and bound rigidly to the hand or forearm
  6. clips: build_models.hero_clips (idle, run, cast, roll, hit, die); the painted A-pose arms are
     brought down to the clips' rest angle with a per-bone offset
Output: godot/models/heroes/<id>.gltf with its textures in <id>_tex/.
"""
import bpy
import bmesh
import json
import math
import os
